Now I see the issue. Looking at `stringifyName` function:

```typescript
function stringifyName(name: string, next: Token | undefined): string {
  if (!ID.test(name)) return quoteName(name);

  if (next?.type === "text" && ID_CONTINUE.test(next.value[0])) {
    return quoteName(name);
  }

  return name;
}
```

The check `ID_CONTINUE.test(next.value[0])` uses `next.value[0]` to get the first character. However, `next.value` is a string. In JavaScript, strings are UTF-16 encoded, so `next.value[0]` will only get the first UTF-16 code unit, not the first Unicode character (code point).

If `next.value` starts with an astral character like `\u{1D6FC}` (which is `ID_Continue`), it's represented in UTF-16 as a surrogate pair: `\uD835\uDECC`. `next.value[0]` would give `\uD835` (a high surrogate), which is not a valid character and doesn't match `ID_CONTINUE`. So the check fails, and the name is not quoted when it should be.

The test case at line 251 actually tests for this case! Let me look at it:

```typescript
{
    // `\u{1D6FC}` is `ID_Continue`, so a bare name would absorb it on re-parse.
    data: new TokenData([
      { type: "text", value: "/" },
      { type: "param", name: "test" },
      { type: "text", value: "\u{1D6FC}" },
    ]),
    expected: '/:"test"\u{1D6FC}',
},
```

So this test case is exactly the bug described. The expected output is `/:"test"\u{1D6FC}` but with the current implementation, `ID_CONTINUE.test(next.value[0])` will test against the high surrogate `\uD835` instead of the astral character `\u{1D6FC}`.

